<h2>The prosecution: fake invoices and a ₹43 crore ITC claim</h2>
<p>The Directorate General of GST Intelligence filed a private complaint before the Special Court for Economic Offences, Bengaluru, alleging offences under Sections 132(1)(b), 132(1)(c) and 132(1)(f) of the CGST Act, 2017. The respondent, proprietor of a trading firm, was accused of issuing invoices without any actual supply of goods and availing fraudulent input tax credit, with the alleged loss to the exchequer computed at over ₹43 crore. A search of his premises reportedly ended in resistance and flight, a separate FIR, and eventually his arrest.</p>

<h2>The procedural tangle</h2>
<p>What followed was less a defence than a campaign across forums, and it is this that drew the Supreme Court's attention.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bail rejected twice.</strong> Successive bail applications failed before the trial court and the Sessions Court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A writ petition filed.</strong> The respondent simultaneously challenged his arrest, seeking release — and the prayer for ad-interim release was refused.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A vacation listing.</strong> A criminal petition was listed before the High Court during the winter vacation on a memo from counsel, without adequate notice to the department.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bail granted.</strong> The Single Judge granted relief — unaware, the Supreme Court found, of the earlier rejections.</div>
</div>
<p>The department carried the matter to the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Parallel remedies need full disclosure</h4><p>The right to challenge an arrest and the right to seek bail both exist. But pursuing them in parallel without telling each court what the other has done is an abuse of process and forum shopping.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Suppression forfeits discretion</h4><p>The High Court was not told of the Sessions Court rejection or the refusal of ad-interim release. The bench described the conduct as lacking <em>uberrima fides</em> — the utmost good faith that a plea for discretionary relief presupposes.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Vacation listings are not a side door</h4><p>Listing a matter in vacation on counsel's memo, without notice to the opposite party, was an attempt to gain an unfair advantage. Such matters should ordinarily await the court's reopening.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The operative directions were calibrated rather than punitive. The Court set aside the portion of the order finally disposing of the criminal petition and revived it before the High Court; the respondent remains on interim bail on the existing terms; the department may file its counter-affidavit within two weeks with a rejoinder to follow; and the Single Judge is to decide the petition on its merits, preferably by the end of the year.</p>

<h2>Guidance for registries on vacation periods</h2>
<p>The judgment also addresses a recurring practical question: what happens when a period fixed by a court — say, two weeks to file objections — expires during a vacation? The Court indicated that the registry should ordinarily exclude the vacation in computing such periods, and that where a bench makes a matter returnable on a date falling within a vacation or directs listing before a Vacation Bench, the registry should list it accordingly, subject to the rules of the High Court concerned.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters in bail practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Disclose every prior order.</strong> A bail application should candidly set out earlier applications, their fate, and any connected writ or quashing proceedings. An omission discovered later can undo relief already granted.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose a track.</strong> Where an arrest is to be challenged as illegal and bail is also to be sought, the sequencing and the disclosure in each proceeding need deliberate thought, not parallel improvisation.</li>
    <li><strong>Urgency must be genuine.</strong> Vacation listings obtained on a memo, without the other side knowing, now carry a visible risk of being unwound.</li>
    <li><strong>The relief survives on terms.</strong> Even where conduct is deprecated, courts may preserve interim liberty while the main matter is heard afresh — as happened here — but no accused should bank on that indulgence.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>For practitioners in Delhi and elsewhere, the case joins a growing line of authority treating candour as a condition of discretionary criminal relief. The strength of a bail plea lies in its merits; its survival lies in its honesty.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Facts of individual cases differ, and the judgment should be read in full before being relied upon.</p></div>
